During slow-wave sleep, the hippocampus 'replays' the day's experiences and transfers them to the neocortex for long-term storage — a process called memory consolidation that cannot occur while awake.
Students who sleep after learning retain 40% more material 24 hours later than those who stay awake, across dozens of controlled experiments (Walker, 2017; Stickgold, 2005).
The glymphatic system — the brain's waste-clearance mechanism — is 10× more active during sleep and removes amyloid-beta plaques, the proteins associated with Alzheimer's disease.
A single night of sleep deprivation increases amyloid-beta accumulation in the brain by 5%, according to a 2017 JAMA Neurology study, linking chronic poor sleep to dementia risk.
